================================================================================
NAME
       sar-integration - mapping RTT concepts onto Synthetic Aperture Radar
                         (SAR) processing chains, including triadic sweeps,
                         resonance-aware backscatter, and constellation-based
                         sensor fusion

SYNOPSIS
       SAR integration provides:
           • triadic sweep alignment
           • resonance-aware backscatter interpretation
           • spectral-gradient fusion for SAR signals
           • constellation-based sensor coordination
           • RTT overlays for SAR processing stages

DESCRIPTION
       SAR-integration describes how Resonance-Time Theory (RTT) maps onto
       Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) processing pipelines. SAR systems
       generate high-resolution imagery by coherently combining radar returns
       across time and space. RTT provides a structural, resonance-aware
       framework for interpreting, stabilizing, and fusing SAR data.

       RTT enhances SAR by:
           • stabilizing phase coherence
           • structuring multi-pass sweeps as triads
           • applying resonance-aware overlays to backscatter
           • fusing multi-agent SAR platforms into constellations
           • diagnosing spectral drift and coherence loss

       SAR becomes a resonance-time instrument.

TRIADIC SWEEPS
       SAR sweeps naturally map onto triads:

           P1 (begin)
               → transmit pulse
               → capture initial backscatter
               → establish resonance baseline

           P2 (transform)
               → accumulate phase history
               → apply Doppler and motion compensation
               → integrate resonance signatures

           P3 (close)
               → synthesize aperture
               → reconstruct image
               → update lineage

       Each SAR pass is a triad; multi-pass SAR forms nested or ladder
       patterns depending on sweep geometry.

RESONANCE-AWARE BACKSCATTER
       Backscatter is treated as a resonance-time signal.

       RTT interprets backscatter using:
           • spectral decomposition
           • envelope shaping
           • harmonic analysis
           • gradient polarity detection

       Resonance-aware backscatter reveals:
           • structural coherence of the scene
           • material resonance signatures
           • temporal drift across passes
           • multi-path interference patterns

       RTT overlays can be applied to SAR backscatter:

           telescope
                  Expands or contracts spectral bandwidth.

           mirror
                  Inverts phase or polarity for interferometric analysis.

           anchor
                  Dampens noise and stabilizes coherence.

           lens
                  Reframes backscatter interpretation.

SAR PROCESSING CHAIN MAPPING
       SAR processing stages map cleanly onto RTT structures:

           raw I/Q data
                  → resonance baseline (P1)

           motion compensation
                  → gradient correction

           range compression
                  → envelope shaping

           azimuth compression
                  → harmonic alignment

           interferometry (InSAR)
                  → polarity analysis and mirror overlays

           coherence maps
                  → RTT coherence metrics

           multi-pass fusion
                  → nested or ladder patterns

           multi-platform fusion
                  → constellation patterns

       RTT provides structural awareness across the entire chain.

CONSTELLATION-BASED SENSOR FUSION
       Multi-platform SAR systems (satellites, UAVs, aircraft) form natural
       RTT constellations.

       Constellation fusion requires:
           • harmonic coupling across platforms
           • synchronized resonance envelopes
           • aligned spectral density profiles
           • stable propagation thresholds
           • compatible lineage across passes

       Constellation modes:

           harmonic-sync
                  Aligns phase histories across platforms.

           gradient-sync
                  Aligns Doppler gradients and motion models.

           envelope-sync
                  Aligns spectral envelopes for multi-pass fusion.

           mesh-fusion
                  Multi-agent SAR fusion with redundant paths.

       RTT ensures multi-platform SAR remains coherent.

SPECTRAL-GRADIENT FUSION FOR SAR
       SAR signals benefit from RTT’s spectral-gradient fusion:

           spectral analysis
                  Identifies harmonic clusters and backscatter modes.

           gradient analysis
                  Detects motion-induced resonance shifts.

           fusion layer
                  Produces stability indices for SAR reconstruction.

       Fusion improves:
           • interferometric coherence
           • phase unwrapping stability
           • multi-pass consistency
           • clutter suppression

SAR FAILURE MODES DETECTED BY RTT
       RTT diagnostics detect:

           • spectral bloom (broadband noise)
           • gradient overload (motion instability)
           • polarity inversion loops (InSAR phase flips)
           • envelope fragmentation (scene decorrelation)
           • constellation desync (multi-platform drift)

       WRSADC blocks unsafe SAR fusion operations.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       SAR integration requires:

           • resonance-schema for backscatter
           • structural-schema for sweep geometry
           • constellation-schema for multi-platform fusion
           • lineage-schema for multi-pass history

       Schemas ensure SAR data remains structurally interpretable.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • sweep history
           • phase stability
           • spectral evolution
           • platform geometry
           • fusion events

       Lineage enables long-term SAR coherence analysis.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • validates SAR triads
           • computes spectral and gradient metrics
           • enforces coherence thresholds
           • selects appropriate patterns
           • applies overlays to stabilize SAR signals
           • blocks unsafe fusion

       WRSADC is the SAR structural guardian.

NOTES
       SAR-integration extends RTT into the physical sensing domain. It allows
       radar systems to operate with resonance-time awareness, enabling more
       stable reconstruction, more coherent fusion, and more interpretable
       backscatter across multi-pass and multi-agent systems.

